  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "No L6 record measures the query molecule; the hepatic-clearance/metabolic-stability bundle contains hepatocyte and microsomal data for reference molecules, so it is mechanistic context rather than a direct oral-bioavailability result.",
      "card_ids": ["C80","C81","C82","C83","C84","C85","C86","C87","C88","C89","C90","C91"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The closest L6 reference, vismodegib (methylsulfonylphenyl/chlorophenyl-pyridine), was metabolically stable in human hepatocytes (96±12% remaining after 3 h; described as very metabolically stable), supporting low first-pass hepatic loss for a related scaffold.",
      "card_ids": ["C80","C82","C85","C88"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Other L6 records with higher intrinsic clearance (C86, C87, C91) are for less similar methylsulfonyl compounds and do not affirmatively establish high first-pass clearance for the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C86","C87","C91"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The most relevant L1/L2 human absolute-bioavailability contexts for methylsulfonylphenyl/heteroaryl scaffolds are all >=20%: esaxerenone 89% fasted, basmisanil ~75%, BI 425809 71.6%, odanacatib 30% at 50 mg, and bitopertin inferred high in humans.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C20","C02","C05","C08"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The only sub-20% values in the visible evidence are bitopertin rat microsuspension values of 14% and 23%, which are low-transferability animal formulation data and do not outweigh the human evidence.",
      "card_ids": ["C31","C35"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4/L5 records for related methylsulfonylphenyl compounds indicate high permeability and no strong efflux (vismodegib high-permeability BCS class II; rofecoxib and BI 730357 high Caco-2 permeability with efflux ratio ~1), supporting adequate absorption.",
      "card_ids": ["C62","C67","C75","C73","C79"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C80","C81","C82","C83","C84","C85","C86","C87","C88","C89","C90","C91"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability or matched oral/IV AUC measurement exists for the query molecule itself.",
    "No query-specific hepatic clearance or metabolic stability measurement is available; L6 records are for reference molecules.",
    "Query-specific solubility and dissolution are not directly measured; high logP and low Fsp3 suggest possible dissolution limitation.",
    "No query-specific gut-wall efflux or intestinal metabolism measurements are available.",
    "Transfer from related methylsulfonylphenyl/heteroaryl compounds is qualitative and does not establish the query's exact F value.",
    "High-clearance L6 records for less similar compounds leave uncertainty about whether the query's methylpyridine/chloropyridine motifs introduce metabolic liability."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains high. L6 adds no query-specific hepatic-clearance result, but the closest related methylsulfonylphenyl/chlorophenyl-pyridine reference (vismodegib) is metabolically stable in human hepatocytes, supporting low first-pass hepatic loss. This strengthens the prior high prediction based on related human absolute bioavailability values of 30-89%; the only sub-20% values are low-transferability rat microsuspension data. Confidence remains moderate because no direct query F or query-specific clearance measurement is available."
}

